amdxdna: stop re-invalidating the whole command aperture per dispatch

`RefreshCommandApertureGpuMapping` implements the legacy path-B "remap
boundary": Unlock2/Lock2 the command aperture so the MCDM miniport observes
freshly staged instruction bytes before the consuming submit. It re-acquired
the mapping through `LockCommandApertureGpuAfterBootstrap`, which also issues a
`D3DKMTInvalidateCache` over the *entire* aperture allocation (64 MiB on
Strix). Every path-B single dispatch therefore paid an allocation-wide cache
invalidate, plus the paging work that its collateral CPU-cache eviction forced
onto each following opcode-9 publication and hardware-queue submit.

That invalidate is bootstrap-only by construction, as the comment on the
`SubmitAndWaitCommandAperture` call site already states: XRT locks and
invalidates the 64 MiB aperture once, after the opcode-2 bootstrap submit.
Every CPU write into the aperture afterwards is published over exactly the
range it touched -- `CopyAndCommitPathBCodeWrites`/`CommitPathBCodeWrites`
(KMT invalidate on the legacy contract) for code and chain descriptors -- so
repeating an allocation-wide invalidate on the remap boundary adds nothing.

Split the plain mapping lock out of the bootstrap helper. Bootstrap keeps
lock + allocation-wide invalidate; the remap boundary keeps the Unlock2/Lock2
lock transition and the caller's range-scoped invalidate, and drops the
allocation-wide one. Instrumenting the re-lock showed it returns the same CPU
pointer on all 13626 remaps of a MoE prefill, so the transition itself is
mapping-neutral and is left in place.

Measured on Windows Strix Halo (1022:17f0), driver 32.0.203.314, interleaved
A/B in one binary, FastFlowLM 1k bench:

| model                  | prefill tok/s | TTFT s |
|------------------------|---------------|--------|
| qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b    | 24.9 -> 34.6  | 39.2 -> 28.2 |
| qwen3:0.6b             |  897 -> 1014  | 1.100 -> 0.973 |
| gemma4-it:e2b          |  460 ->  515  | 2.135 -> 1.903 |

MoE amplifies the win because it issues ~13.7k single dispatches per 1k-token
prefill; the per-dispatch aperture stage cost drops from 8.7s to 6.1s and the
publish/submit phases from 1.4s/2.7s to 0.8s/1.0s. Dense models gain ~12%
prefill for the same reason at lower dispatch counts.

Apply on top of: bb10dfb81 ("amdxdna: treat Windows UMDRIVERPRIVATE as adapter
info")

Tested: Windows Strix Halo (1022:17f0), driver 32.0.203.314. `dev.py
test-fastflowlm` smoke PASS with coherent multi-turn output; qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b
1k x3, qwen3.5:0.8b 1k+4k x5, qwen3:0.6b 1k x5, gemma4-it:e2b 1k x5 all PASS
with zero `ert state 8` timeouts.
